# Large-level parabolic dimension count for central-fixed coinduced coefficients

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.centralCoinduced_parabolicH1_dimension_upper_bound`
(`0c63643a-996a-4912-8a0d-34924c75dff7`), by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08.

Proof: submission `b201d71a-57f3-431f-9e33-021a45069e3a`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $N\ge5$ and $k\ge3$. Put $G=\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$, $H=\Gamma_1(N)$,
$V=\operatorname{Sym}^{k-2}\mathbb C^2$, and $W^+=(\operatorname{Coind}_H^G V)^{-I=1}$. Let
$\mu=[G:\langle H,-I\rangle]$ and $c=\#(H\backslash G/\langle T,-I\rangle)$, where
$T=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. Then $$6\dim_{\mathbb
C}H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(G,W^+)+6c\le(k-1)\mu.$$ The parabolic condition uses all rational-cusp
stabilizers. This is the independent cohomological dimension count on the full modular group, with
the central action already made trivial. Together with the parabolic Shapiro comparison, it gives
the MTT large-level upper bound. The restrictions $N\ge5$ exclude the small-level elliptic and
irregular-cusp corrections.

## Explanation of the source proof

# Only the finite-coset dimension counts remain

Write $d=\dim W^+$ and let $s,u,t$ be the dimensions fixed by $S,ST,T$.
The new closed theorem `325f1489-247a-46c6-9aa1-f960c954fdd9` proves

$$\dim H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(G,W^+)+s+u+t\le d.$$

The explicit remaining child `bb6b5766-fb31-4de5-9462-40a64feda1a8` asserts
$d=(n+1)\mu$, $2s=d$, $3u=d$, and $c\le t$. Multiplying the first inequality
by six and substituting those relations gives

$$6\dim H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(G,W^+)+6c\le d=(n+1)\mu.$$

Taking $n=k-2$, with $k\ge3$, gives the parent statement. The arithmetic
combination is checked in Lean. The invariant/coinvariant vanishing and the
cohomology-model comparison are proved independently in the closed theorem;
they are no longer part of the open frontier.

The remaining finite-dimensional assertion is not claimed proved here. Its
intended proof uses coset coordinates for coinduction: the central involution
pairs cosets; the induced $S$ and $ST$ actions on central pairs have free
orbits of lengths two and three at $N\ge5$; and each cusp orbit contributes a
unipotent fixed line for $T$. The absence of elliptic and irregular-cusp
corrections must be established using the actual Gamma1 congruence conditions.
No assertion for Gamma0 in even weight is silently reused for this step.

